1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:11,840
From a simple billy club to an arsenal of weapons designed to snip out the tiniest clue or knock down the toughest bad guy.

2
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:16,280
Now, Police Technology on Modern Marvels.

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Clad in full body armor and ballistic helmets, with goggles that can see through darkness

4
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:50,600
and smoke, and weapons that can stun, shock and disable, today's SWAT teams are the embodiment

5
00:00:50,601 --> 00:00:54,200
of high tech cops.

6
00:00:54,201 --> 00:00:58,120
And the future is just around the corner.

7
00:00:58,121 --> 00:01:04,200
The Urban Robot is a surveillance device designed to go places no human would dare.

8
00:01:04,201 --> 00:01:11,000
Able to climb stairs, maneuver through tight spaces, open doors, and even blow a hole through

9
00:01:11,001 --> 00:01:14,040
a wall.

10
00:01:14,041 --> 00:01:20,700
It has video, audio, and even infrared sensors for letting those on the outside know what's

11
00:01:20,701 --> 00:01:23,200
going on inside.

12
00:01:23,201 --> 00:01:30,200
In case of danger, it can hide from sight or escape to safety from a second-story window.

13
00:01:30,201 --> 00:01:39,200
The urban robot may or may not be the policeman of the future, but it is certainly a considerable

14
00:01:39,201 --> 00:01:46,200
change from the policeman of the past. The precursors to modern-day police date back

15
00:01:46,201 --> 00:01:49,787
at least as far as the Roman Empire's Praetorian Guard.

16
00:01:49,788 --> 00:01:52,200
Essentially a private army, they were

17
00:01:52,201 --> 00:01:59,000
army, they wielded their swords to protect the emperor and do his dirty work. In the

18
00:01:59,001 --> 00:02:03,980
Middle Ages, nobles appointed local constables to uphold the law, but for the most part,

19
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:06,363
they were unpaid and unarmed.

20
00:02:06,364 --> 00:02:11,080
In 17th century France, Louis XIV established a small central

21
00:02:11,081 --> 00:02:17,940
police force whose main job was to spy on dissidents and, when necessary, suppress them.

22
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:24,620
But the first true police force as we know it wasn't formed until 1829 in London, England.

23
00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:29,640
Their purpose, singular purpose, was to make sure that people behaved themselves in public.

24
00:02:30,980 --> 00:02:35,800
And they would deal with crimes as they occurred, but they weren't created to be proactive to deal with crime.

25
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:39,860
Dubbed Bobbies after their founder Sir Robert Peel,

26
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:43,420
they were armed with little more than a short wooden billy club.

27
00:02:43,421 --> 00:02:49,920
To this day, the Billy Club remains the only weapon carried by the majority of London police.

28
00:02:51,340 --> 00:02:56,560
In the United States, police trace their beginnings to night patrols formed in the mid-1600s,

29
00:02:56,840 --> 00:02:58,560
like New York's Rattle Watch.

30
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:03,100
They were called the Rattle Watch because the officers that were assigned to this watch

31
00:03:03,101 --> 00:03:08,180
carried large wooden rattles that were used to warn the residents of any type of danger.

32
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:12,540
The rattles made an extremely loud noise, much like a machine gun would sound today.

33
00:03:12,541 --> 00:03:15,160
The Rattle Watch used lanterns to patrol at night

34
00:03:15,161 --> 00:03:16,520
because there were no streetlights or anything

35
00:03:16,521 --> 00:03:17,620
of that sort at the time.

36
00:03:17,940 --> 00:03:19,040
It would illuminate their path.

37
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:21,480
It would also allow citizens in the area

38
00:03:21,481 --> 00:03:24,640
to find the watchmen if they needed them very quickly.

39
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:28,680
In 1820, they were replaced by a fire brigade

40
00:03:28,681 --> 00:03:30,840
that became known as the Leatherheads.

41
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:32,920
The reason why they were called Leatherheads

42
00:03:32,921 --> 00:03:35,020
is because they wore a large leather helmet,

43
00:03:35,640 --> 00:03:38,640
which had a large piece of leather draped down the back,

44
00:03:38,740 --> 00:03:40,820
much like a firefighter's helmet would be today.

45
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:45,480
In addition to fighting fires, they were also expected to fight crime.

46
00:03:46,220 --> 00:03:50,800
But New York's booming and unruly population soon proved to be more than they could handle.

47
00:03:52,140 --> 00:03:56,820
In 1845, New York City established its first official police department.

48
00:03:57,820 --> 00:04:02,360
Their copper badges led to the nickname Coppers, later shortened to Cops.

49
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:06,960
They carried devices that were the predecessor to handcuffs called nippers

50
00:04:06,961 --> 00:04:09,180
that would be twisted around a person's wrists

51
00:04:09,181 --> 00:04:11,700
and held by the officer to control their hands.

52
00:04:12,660 --> 00:04:16,020
Like their British counterparts, they were armed with only a nightstick.

53
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:20,340
It was thought that an officer who needed a firearm could not be a good officer.

54
00:04:20,540 --> 00:04:23,560
I mean, if you had to resort to shooting someone, obviously you're doing something wrong.

55
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:28,480
But that opinion changed when a series of draft riots in the 1870s

56
00:04:28,481 --> 00:04:33,340
made police and the public realize how quickly situations could get out of control.

57
00:04:33,341 --> 00:04:37,060
It made natural sense that officers should be prepared to protect themselves.

58
00:04:37,420 --> 00:04:40,940
If you meet a crowd of ruffians and you hit the first couple with a nightstick,

59
00:04:41,060 --> 00:04:43,840
that leaves the rest of the crowd to beat up on you.

60
00:04:44,780 --> 00:04:49,100
In the 1870s, officers were given permission to carry their own guns,

61
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:52,680
hidden beneath long frock coats so as not to alarm the public.

62
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:59,040
It was not until 1887 that the NYPD issued its first official firearm,

63
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:01,280
a Colt .32 revolver.

64
00:05:01,281 --> 00:05:07,520
The daily work of the vast majority of these turn-of-the-century policemen was to walk the beat.

65
00:05:07,521 --> 00:05:11,120
The beat was a geographical area that they were assigned to.

66
00:05:11,121 --> 00:05:16,120
Other than arresting violators that they encountered and responding to calls for help,

67
00:05:16,121 --> 00:05:19,520
for assistance from officers, they were there as problem solvers as well.

68
00:05:19,521 --> 00:05:20,627
The officer was your friend.

69
00:05:20,628 --> 00:05:22,920
If you had a problem, you would go to the officer because,

70
00:05:22,921 --> 00:05:25,257
hey, he had a uniform. That's his job is to help you.

71
00:05:25,258 --> 00:05:26,800
If there's a crime being committed,

72
00:05:26,801 --> 00:05:29,120
you would go for the police officer. If you had a question, if you had a problem,

73
00:05:29,121 --> 00:05:32,340
or Mrs. Smith went into labor, you'd go find a police officer.

74
00:05:32,460 --> 00:05:33,200
They were there to help you.

75
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:35,060
Important part of the community at this point.

76
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:39,800
He had no means of communications back to headquarters until later in the century.

77
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:43,000
And so his way of communicating, if he needed help,

78
00:05:43,380 --> 00:05:48,720
was to wrap his baton hard on the concrete and hope that someone would hear it.

79
00:05:49,860 --> 00:05:55,200
But in the late 1800s, the newly invented telephone was put to one of its first practical uses

80
00:05:55,201 --> 00:05:58,760
with the introduction of the call box, or street telephone,

81
00:05:59,120 --> 00:06:03,220
This gave beat cops their first real link back to their precinct house.

82
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,260
That was a dramatic improvement over beating on the concrete.

83
00:06:06,720 --> 00:06:08,460
You could go to the call box, pick it up.

84
00:06:08,540 --> 00:06:10,460
There was a direct line that went straight to the police.

85
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:14,000
Call boxes really sort of revolutionized beat patrol work.

86
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:16,080
You could easily call back to dispatch.

87
00:06:16,260 --> 00:06:19,300
You could easily get help if you go to the call box, open it up,

88
00:06:19,301 --> 00:06:21,400
and you could get to someone in a hurry.

89
00:06:22,520 --> 00:06:26,700
In many cities, call boxes remained in use until the 1960s.

90
00:06:26,701 --> 00:06:31,840
They were operated with a key, which I was assigned, which had my shield number stamped on it.

91
00:06:32,380 --> 00:06:41,000
And at predetermined times, I was expected to open that box with my key, pick the receiver off,

92
00:06:41,060 --> 00:06:44,580
wait for somebody to pick up the other end and tell them that, you know,

93
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:49,600
Patrolman Flynn, you know, beat number 14, 1745 badge number, on post.

94
00:06:51,180 --> 00:06:54,820
The first vehicles used by police were dubbed paddy wagons,

95
00:06:54,821 --> 00:06:58,120
most likely a slur against their frequent Irish passengers.

96
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:03,180
Used for transporting prisoners, they were originally open supply wagons,

97
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:05,200
left over from the Spanish-American War.

98
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:08,340
But most soon added covers,

99
00:07:08,780 --> 00:07:12,820
reportedly to protect passengers from excessive publicity and humiliation.

100
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:19,100
To combat the problem of high-speed carriages and runaway steeds,

101
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:22,000
some cities began putting officers on horseback.

102
00:07:22,001 --> 00:07:27,000
In 1895, New York announced the formation of a Scorcher Squad.

103
00:07:27,001 --> 00:07:30,000
They were 25 men on high-speed bicycles.

104
00:07:30,001 --> 00:07:36,000
The reason why they obtained the name of the Scorcher Squad was because from their smoking wheels of going so quickly.

105
00:07:36,001 --> 00:07:45,000
They were pretty much established to combat the rising traffic condition with the advent of automobiles into New York City.

106
00:07:45,001 --> 00:07:51,000
To keep pace with faster cars, bikes were soon replaced by motorcycles.

107
00:07:51,001 --> 00:07:54,800
And it wasn't long before police got automobiles of their own.

108
00:07:55,580 --> 00:07:58,880
These first police cars were notable for their political sensitivity.

109
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:02,200
Sirens weren't allowed because they scared people.

110
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:06,460
Some departments required officers to ride in open-top convertibles.

111
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:11,240
In the early part of the use of patrol guards by the New York State Police,

112
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:16,100
they bought convertibles because it was felt that if you drive around in a car that has a ceiling on it,

113
00:08:16,420 --> 00:08:19,260
people are going to lose touch with you as a state trooper.

114
00:08:19,900 --> 00:08:22,140
Now, Western New York, as I said, is very cold.

115
00:08:22,420 --> 00:08:26,520
Imagine being a police officer in the 1930s driving around with the flaps up on their coats

116
00:08:26,521 --> 00:08:30,600
and big bulky hats on and goggles virtually driving.

117
00:08:31,360 --> 00:08:33,140
It's very amusing stuff.

118
00:08:34,660 --> 00:08:38,720
By the 1930s, police cars began to be equipped with radios.

119
00:08:39,080 --> 00:08:41,120
The first car radios were one-way radios.

120
00:08:41,720 --> 00:08:45,040
One-way radio, the dispatcher would contact the patrol car,

121
00:08:45,240 --> 00:08:47,220
request them to contact the precinct station house.

122
00:08:47,221 --> 00:08:51,760
they would then have to go to a call box and confirm that they received the message

123
00:08:51,761 --> 00:08:54,400
because they weren't able to communicate back through that same radio.

124
00:08:55,240 --> 00:08:58,340
As cars became more and more ubiquitous in police departments,

125
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:00,680
you could dispatch police from a central point.

126
00:09:00,840 --> 00:09:02,460
This is efficient.

127
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,940
It means you can send a police officer wherever they're needed

128
00:09:05,941 --> 00:09:09,720
and you don't have to have a police officer who is basically isolated,

129
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:11,820
trapped to a beat to walk only that beat.

130
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:14,700
That sounds very good and very efficient.

131
00:09:14,701 --> 00:09:21,240
The problem is that it meant now that police officers were in cars responding to calls for service.

132
00:09:21,900 --> 00:09:25,600
What they weren't doing now was walking the beat, getting to know the neighborhood.

133
00:09:26,060 --> 00:09:29,140
It went from being Officer Smith, who is our police officer,

134
00:09:29,141 --> 00:09:33,920
to being a nameless, faceless person in a patrol car, usually two at this time period,

135
00:09:34,440 --> 00:09:37,980
driving around and stopping only when they saw indications of criminal behavior.

136
00:09:38,480 --> 00:09:42,700
By the end of the Second World War, police technology had come a long way.

137
00:09:42,701 --> 00:09:46,740
But police were soon to find out how far they still had to go.

138
00:09:48,080 --> 00:09:52,240
In Houston, police calls were originally broadcast by local radio stations,

139
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:55,060
which interrupted programming for police emergencies.

140
00:09:58,660 --> 00:10:01,920
As criminals got better armed, so did the police.

141
00:10:02,500 --> 00:10:07,520
As early as the 1930s, rifles and shotguns began to be a standard part of the police arsenal.

142
00:10:07,521 --> 00:10:16,560
In the 1960s, departments slowly began replacing the six-shot revolver with semi-automatic pistols that could hold up to 16 rounds.

143
00:10:18,300 --> 00:10:25,020
But despite these arsenals, in the early 1960s, urban unrest and crime started to increase rapidly.

144
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:30,200
Crime had taken a dramatic upturn in the mid-60s.

145
00:10:30,201 --> 00:10:35,500
In fact, if you go back and look at statistics in the Census Bureau and other places,

146
00:10:35,501 --> 00:10:40,260
you see this remarkable uptick in crime beginning about 1965.

147
00:10:41,160 --> 00:10:45,400
In 1965, President Lyndon Johnson convened a presidential commission

148
00:10:45,401 --> 00:10:49,960
on law enforcement and administration, better known as the Crime Commission.

149
00:10:51,140 --> 00:10:54,220
That commission looked at how police worked,

150
00:10:54,740 --> 00:10:57,660
and it had a piece that looked at science and technology as well.

151
00:10:57,661 --> 00:11:03,160
Its role was to say, how can we as a nation begin to address this problem?

152
00:11:03,161 --> 00:11:05,320
And one of their answers was technology.

153
00:11:05,321 --> 00:11:08,660
We need to have more technology, we need to make better use of technology,

154
00:11:08,661 --> 00:11:13,200
we need to pass that technology on to the officers that are in the field.

155
00:11:13,201 --> 00:11:22,320
Policing in 1960 was essentially what it was, technology speaking, in the early 1900s.

156
00:11:22,321 --> 00:11:24,960
If you carried any technology at all, it was a handheld radio,

157
00:11:24,961 --> 00:11:26,820
and you may have arrived in a car instead of a horse.

158
00:11:26,821 --> 00:11:28,820
Those were the key differences.

159
00:11:28,821 --> 00:11:31,820
And at the time the Crime Commission report came forward,

160
00:11:31,821 --> 00:11:34,820
they noted all these dramatic changes in technology

161
00:11:34,821 --> 00:11:35,820
in the general society,

162
00:11:35,821 --> 00:11:38,820
but very few such changes in law enforcement.

163
00:11:38,821 --> 00:11:40,820
One of the commission's recommendations

164
00:11:40,821 --> 00:11:44,820
was to create a single nationwide emergency telephone number.

165
00:11:44,821 --> 00:11:47,820
Some of the audience will remember when they were younger,

166
00:11:47,821 --> 00:11:49,820
before the days of 9-1-1,

167
00:11:49,821 --> 00:11:52,820
when you had the number of the police department taped next to the telephone.

168
00:11:52,821 --> 00:11:55,820
And wherever you happened to be, that number was different.

169
00:11:55,821 --> 00:12:00,100
9-1-1, the concept of having a single emergency number, actually came from Great Britain.

170
00:12:00,600 --> 00:12:02,560
And I think the numbers they use is 9-9-9.

171
00:12:03,420 --> 00:12:09,400
AT&T, the giant monopoly that it was in the 60s and 70s, decided to use 9-1-1 as the number.

172
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:11,220
They thought it was very easy for people to remember.

173
00:12:11,780 --> 00:12:17,100
It wasn't a single set of digits, so you can't have children inadvertently click 1-1-1 or 9-9-9.

174
00:12:18,280 --> 00:12:23,740
In early 1968, an Alabama phone company office manager named Bill Fry

175
00:12:23,741 --> 00:12:27,280
rerouted local lines to enable 911 dialing.

176
00:12:28,120 --> 00:12:33,980
On February 16, 1968, the first 911 call in the U.S. was made

177
00:12:33,981 --> 00:12:36,440
from the small town of Haleyville, Alabama.

178
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:42,360
That same year, New York became the first major city to install a 911 system.

179
00:12:43,900 --> 00:12:49,040
Over the next three decades, 911 spread to over 85% of the country.

180
00:12:50,120 --> 00:12:53,380
Most current systems use enhanced 911 technology,

181
00:12:53,740 --> 00:12:56,680
which displays the caller's name and address automatically.

182
00:12:57,200 --> 00:12:57,900
Okay.

183
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:03,280
In this country alone, the system now fields over 100 million calls every year.

184
00:13:04,480 --> 00:13:08,200
Another result of the crime commission was a flood of federal grants

185
00:13:08,201 --> 00:13:10,420
to equip police departments with computers.

186
00:13:11,620 --> 00:13:13,340
Computerized equipment was very popular.

187
00:13:13,400 --> 00:13:14,100
That was a big thing.

188
00:13:14,220 --> 00:13:16,740
Let's give police departments money to buy their own computers.

189
00:13:17,180 --> 00:13:19,820
It wound up being a terrible fiasco.

190
00:13:19,821 --> 00:13:24,820
There were instances of computers, whole mainframes or mini computers,

191
00:13:24,821 --> 00:13:27,820
that were purchased and installed in apartments and never turned on

192
00:13:27,821 --> 00:13:30,820
because they didn't have people to operate them.

193
00:13:30,821 --> 00:13:32,820
You have a bunch of officers who are like, well, it's a computer.

194
00:13:32,821 --> 00:13:34,820
What do I know about ones and zeros?

195
00:13:34,821 --> 00:13:36,820
Mini computers wound up sitting in rooms,

196
00:13:36,821 --> 00:13:38,820
very expensive filing cabinets.

197
00:13:38,821 --> 00:13:41,820
So it was just terrible.

198
00:13:41,821 --> 00:13:44,820
I think the technology was still in its infancy,

199
00:13:44,821 --> 00:13:48,820
and any technology that's going to have a police application

200
00:13:48,821 --> 00:13:50,440
has to be robust.

201
00:13:52,920 --> 00:13:55,780
Research and development efforts by the Justice Department

202
00:13:55,781 --> 00:13:58,020
quickly dwindled down to a single man

203
00:13:58,021 --> 00:14:01,720
who was destined to become one of the great pioneers of police technology,

204
00:14:02,640 --> 00:14:03,920
chemist Lester Shubin.

205
00:14:04,600 --> 00:14:07,000
I got into it partly because

206
00:14:07,001 --> 00:14:10,700
maybe it's just my mind.

207
00:14:12,300 --> 00:14:14,860
Nobody else seemed to be picking some of these things up.

208
00:14:14,861 --> 00:14:19,360
One of Shubin's first ideas was inspired by his pet dog.

209
00:14:19,760 --> 00:14:22,460
I had a hunting dog that had been trained,

210
00:14:22,860 --> 00:14:25,220
and I used to throw a stick out into the woods,

211
00:14:25,740 --> 00:14:28,560
and even if I was wearing gloves, he'd bring the same stick back,

212
00:14:28,620 --> 00:14:30,140
and I thought if a dog could do that,

213
00:14:31,420 --> 00:14:33,520
you know, a thousand other sticks out there just like it,

214
00:14:33,660 --> 00:14:35,620
if he could bring back the same stick all the time,

215
00:14:35,700 --> 00:14:37,300
then they ought to be able to do something else.

216
00:14:38,020 --> 00:14:39,860
Well, I went to the Army at Aberdeen,

217
00:14:40,240 --> 00:14:42,960
and we worked out a training system,

218
00:14:42,961 --> 00:14:48,500
and we found, remarkably, they could keep finding these explosives

219
00:14:48,501 --> 00:14:51,100
and almost 100% success.

220
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:55,860
Despite the successful results, few took Shubin's dogs seriously.

221
00:14:56,400 --> 00:14:57,840
I was the butt of some jokes.

222
00:14:58,400 --> 00:15:00,400
What are you going to do when the contract ends?

223
00:15:00,740 --> 00:15:01,940
Where are you going to put the dogs?

224
00:15:03,600 --> 00:15:07,320
And I said, well, I think we're going to kennel them down here in personnel.

225
00:15:08,920 --> 00:15:11,920
And so they decided to give the dogs to New York City.

226
00:15:12,960 --> 00:15:18,260
In March 1972, shortly after a TWA jet took off from Kennedy Airport,

227
00:15:18,920 --> 00:15:22,480
authorities received a phone call informing them that a bomb was on board.

228
00:15:23,580 --> 00:15:26,860
The caller demanded $2 million to reveal its location.

229
00:15:28,020 --> 00:15:30,320
The jet immediately returned to Kennedy Airport,

230
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:34,480
where Brandy, one of Shubin's dogs, found the bomb in just 12 minutes.

231
00:15:35,500 --> 00:15:39,760
The positive publicity allowed Shubin the freedom to explore other ideas.

232
00:15:39,761 --> 00:15:43,060
Well, Lester early on saw an advertisement in a magazine,

233
00:15:43,320 --> 00:15:47,080
and in this ad it had somebody hanging from a single strand of cord,

234
00:15:47,440 --> 00:15:50,860
and it said Kevlar is stronger than steel.

235
00:15:51,220 --> 00:15:55,280
Well, Lester was intrigued by this idea.

236
00:15:56,260 --> 00:16:00,700
Kevlar was being developed by DuPont for use in steel belted radial tires.

237
00:16:01,380 --> 00:16:05,720
I wondered at that time if you had something like this that would stop a bullet.

238
00:16:06,360 --> 00:16:09,520
We got some material, folded it up, took it out.

239
00:16:09,760 --> 00:16:12,320
Shot it, and the bullets bounced off.

240
00:16:15,120 --> 00:16:18,220
Bulletproof garments had been around since the 1930s.

241
00:16:21,360 --> 00:16:24,200
But they were made of metal plates or heavy nylon

242
00:16:24,201 --> 00:16:26,840
and were impractical for everyday use.

243
00:16:31,560 --> 00:16:33,960
Though Kevlar vests were lighter and thinner,

244
00:16:34,400 --> 00:16:36,500
at first few police wanted to wear them.

245
00:16:36,501 --> 00:16:41,240
Their response was, it's bulky, it's hot, it's expensive, it's impractical,

246
00:16:41,340 --> 00:16:43,600
and besides, it's not macho, I'm not going to wear this stuff.

247
00:16:44,140 --> 00:16:45,660
And so they didn't want anything to do with it.

248
00:16:46,260 --> 00:16:50,040
So Lester's approach then was to find enough funding

249
00:16:50,041 --> 00:16:54,940
to manufacture 5,000 units of body armor.

250
00:16:55,340 --> 00:16:57,800
Put them on police, we're providing it free as a test.

251
00:16:58,580 --> 00:17:04,700
In late 1975, Shubin's vests were given away to police departments in 15 cities.

252
00:17:05,420 --> 00:17:11,960
Two days before Christmas, a Seattle patrolman named Ray Johnson was shot twice at near point-blank range.

253
00:17:13,480 --> 00:17:16,140
Fortunately, he was wearing one of Shubin's vests.

254
00:17:16,720 --> 00:17:21,220
I got a call that it happened. They kept the officer in the hospital overnight.

255
00:17:22,220 --> 00:17:25,320
He was very unhappy because he missed Christmas at home.

256
00:17:25,780 --> 00:17:29,520
And he had two deep bruises on his chest, but he survived the shot.

257
00:17:30,040 --> 00:17:34,280
That incident convinced police across the country of the value of body armor.

258
00:17:34,700 --> 00:17:41,620
By the end of the century, Shubin's best had been credited for saving the lives of well over 2,500 officers.

259
00:17:42,640 --> 00:17:49,060
Today, they are lighter, more effective, and appropriately also available for dogs, all thanks to Lester Shubin.

260
00:17:49,380 --> 00:17:54,940
The legacy he left, that is a way of developing a technology, proving that it actually works,

261
00:17:55,380 --> 00:18:01,500
ensuring its quality and getting in the field, is in fact a model that, somewhat enhanced, we still use.

262
00:18:01,501 --> 00:18:07,460
So he really established the foundation of how to develop a technology for the law enforcement community.

263
00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:12,440
Coming up, a Supreme Court decision that led to a revolution in detective work.

264
00:18:14,240 --> 00:18:18,220
The second place in the U.S. to have 911 was Nome, Alaska.

265
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:29,240
In 1963, a 23-year-old truck driver named Ernesto Miranda was arrested in Arizona on charges of kidnapping and rape.

266
00:18:29,241 --> 00:18:34,340
After two hours of questioning, he confessed to both crimes.

267
00:18:35,320 --> 00:18:37,060
Miranda's confession was nothing unusual.

268
00:18:37,840 --> 00:18:42,820
It had become an unwritten rule of police work that the best way to get a conviction was to get a confession.

269
00:18:43,760 --> 00:18:48,200
In 1967, the Supreme Court said that had to change.

270
00:18:48,201 --> 00:18:56,740
The Supreme Court in the Miranda case said officers are being taught how to manipulate people into saying things against their interests.

271
00:18:56,741 --> 00:19:00,240
not unlike a good salesman or a good attorney.

272
00:19:00,740 --> 00:19:02,620
And what the Supreme Court said is, no, wait a minute.

273
00:19:03,140 --> 00:19:05,580
You know, there's certain aspects that are probably okay,

274
00:19:06,020 --> 00:19:09,160
but everybody in this game has to at least start

275
00:19:09,161 --> 00:19:10,860
on a reasonably level playing field,

276
00:19:10,940 --> 00:19:12,580
and that's where this warning is going to come in.

277
00:19:12,700 --> 00:19:13,780
You have the right to remain silent.

278
00:19:14,200 --> 00:19:15,860
You have the right to an attorney, et cetera, et cetera.

279
00:19:16,240 --> 00:19:18,200
And officers were told, you have to say these things.

280
00:19:18,520 --> 00:19:21,120
And if you don't, the bad guy may be let go.

281
00:19:22,260 --> 00:19:24,100
Huge change in how police work took place.

282
00:19:24,540 --> 00:19:26,100
And it actually helped, in some respects,

283
00:19:26,101 --> 00:19:32,000
to professionalize police work because officers had to learn how to use the law properly.

284
00:19:32,120 --> 00:19:33,380
They had to collect good evidence.

285
00:19:34,680 --> 00:19:38,960
Miranda created a demand for better evidence-gathering techniques and technologies.

286
00:19:39,780 --> 00:19:45,423
In the 1970s alone, fostered by federal grants, the number of crime labs in the U.S.

287
00:19:45,424 --> 00:19:46,124
doubled.

288
00:19:48,040 --> 00:19:51,320
Evidence-gathering technology dates back to the late 1800s,

289
00:19:51,321 --> 00:19:55,160
when the Bertel-Lahn system was introduced as a scientific means of recording

290
00:19:55,161 --> 00:19:57,200
and determining a person's true identity.

291
00:19:58,240 --> 00:20:00,600
That system relied on precise measurements

292
00:20:00,601 --> 00:20:02,260
of a suspect's bone structure.

293
00:20:03,100 --> 00:20:06,280
It was really based primarily on the thought

294
00:20:06,281 --> 00:20:08,360
that people's bones did not continue to grow

295
00:20:08,361 --> 00:20:10,920
after the age of 20, which we know it from.

296
00:20:12,140 --> 00:20:13,640
By the turn of the century,

297
00:20:13,840 --> 00:20:16,020
it had been abandoned in favor of a new method

298
00:20:16,021 --> 00:20:18,340
developed by the British, fingerprinting.

299
00:20:19,520 --> 00:20:21,840
The FBI lab in Washington, D.C.

300
00:20:21,940 --> 00:20:24,720
amassed a huge collection of prints of convicted criminals.

301
00:20:25,160 --> 00:20:33,440
But when it came to determining who an unidentified fingerprint belonged to, the technology had

302
00:20:33,441 --> 00:20:34,440
its drawbacks.

303
00:20:34,441 --> 00:20:45,100
I think a generation of people grew up thinking that if we got a fingerprint at a crime scene,

304
00:20:45,101 --> 00:20:48,760
we could find out who committed the crime.

305
00:20:48,761 --> 00:20:50,160
I thought that's the way it worked.

306
00:20:50,161 --> 00:20:57,760
I went to the police academy, and I was astounded to find out that, you know, fingerprints were virtually useless unless we had an identified suspect.

307
00:20:58,320 --> 00:21:02,780
If you have fingerprints from a suspect and you have 60,000 fingerprint records,

308
00:21:02,781 --> 00:21:09,400
a person, a single person, has to go through manually through every single print on file until they come across one.

309
00:21:10,120 --> 00:21:16,020
In the 1970s, the FBI introduced AFIS, the Automated Fingerprint Identification System.

310
00:21:16,520 --> 00:21:18,980
All of a sudden, fingerprinting took on a much bigger turn.

311
00:21:18,981 --> 00:21:25,180
The FIS, which would allow fingerprints to be taken from a suspect and then fed into a system,

312
00:21:25,580 --> 00:21:29,040
which would use a computer database and a computer to analyze the submissions

313
00:21:29,041 --> 00:21:32,620
and compare them very quickly to files that were on record.

314
00:21:33,260 --> 00:21:39,660
And bottom line, it means that criminals that heretofore would have walked away

315
00:21:39,661 --> 00:21:44,280
and potentially fled a jurisdiction will now be arrested.

316
00:21:44,281 --> 00:21:51,280
In L.A., they found a murderer in half an hour going through X amount of records.

317
00:21:51,380 --> 00:21:54,760
It would have taken a technician 67 years to do it by hand.

318
00:21:56,160 --> 00:21:59,640
Gathering fingerprints at a crime scene was also a tedious process

319
00:21:59,641 --> 00:22:03,040
that entailed brushing surfaces with a graphite powder.

320
00:22:03,800 --> 00:22:08,100
The problem with that kind of approach is that if it's a very faint print, it may not pick it up.

321
00:22:08,101 --> 00:22:14,220
If you brush the material on too aggressively, it may brush the print away, and so you may lose it.

322
00:22:14,280 --> 00:22:23,480
In the 1970s, the Japanese developed a way of recovering prints using cyanoacrylate acid, a chemical found in super glue.

323
00:22:24,620 --> 00:22:30,500
Objects were placed in a sealed chamber that was then filled with a super glue fume that made the prints visible.

324
00:22:31,400 --> 00:22:37,220
More recently, vapor wands have been developed that allow investigators to use this technique in the field.

325
00:22:37,221 --> 00:22:45,540
One version is a little bitty wand, almost like a small handheld propane torch that's used to fume things locally.

326
00:22:45,720 --> 00:22:52,440
That can do a number of prints, especially prints in very awkward places where the powder doesn't work real well, like underneath a brass rail.

327
00:22:53,260 --> 00:22:58,700
In the late 1980s, another form of evidence began to be recognized by the courts, DNA.

328
00:22:59,200 --> 00:23:06,440
DNA is probably one of our most important and most interesting technologies now because it works so powerfully in both directions.

329
00:23:06,441 --> 00:23:11,440
It helps catch the guilty and it helps make sure we don't inadvertently convict the innocent.

330
00:23:11,441 --> 00:23:17,440
DNA technologies increasingly are useful in any part of the human body we get,

331
00:23:17,441 --> 00:23:22,440
whether it's hair, fingerprints, blood, saliva, virtually anything we can get off a body

332
00:23:22,441 --> 00:23:24,440
or that's left by a body we can get.

333
00:23:24,441 --> 00:23:29,440
Since bodies, the human body is shedding thousands of cells constantly,

334
00:23:29,441 --> 00:23:32,440
it's very difficult to go anywhere without leaving some trace behind

335
00:23:32,441 --> 00:23:36,560
that might be useful in a forensic setting.

336
00:23:37,120 --> 00:23:39,160
DNA is an important part of forensic technology

337
00:23:39,161 --> 00:23:41,080
because it's virtually inescapable.

338
00:23:41,440 --> 00:23:43,640
If you have crime scene evidence with DNA

339
00:23:43,641 --> 00:23:47,080
and you take DNA evidence from a suspect and the two match,

340
00:23:47,620 --> 00:23:50,120
it's a one in a million chance that it's not correct,

341
00:23:50,640 --> 00:23:54,140
far beyond the possible incorrectness of fingerprints.

342
00:23:55,520 --> 00:23:58,300
In the near future, it will be possible to gather

343
00:23:58,301 --> 00:24:00,560
and analyze DNA samples in the field

344
00:24:00,561 --> 00:24:03,300
using this credit card-sized miniature lab.

345
00:24:04,140 --> 00:24:07,180
A drop of DNA evidence dissolved in a solution

346
00:24:07,181 --> 00:24:10,920
is placed on a microchip that is able to read the DNA fingerprint,

347
00:24:11,500 --> 00:24:14,860
which can then be sent to a central database for comparison.

348
00:24:17,700 --> 00:24:21,080
But technology's greatest contribution to evidence collection

349
00:24:21,081 --> 00:24:25,240
has to do with new ways of storing it and then finding it again.

350
00:24:25,820 --> 00:24:28,640
When I first started out, most of our information

351
00:24:28,641 --> 00:24:33,280
was handwritten on a paper report and stored in filing cabinets.

352
00:24:34,060 --> 00:24:38,100
Well, if I had a name, if I wanted to see what kind of information I had on a person,

353
00:24:38,101 --> 00:24:40,960
or if I had an address, if I wanted to find out information

354
00:24:40,961 --> 00:24:45,020
about any previous police activity at this address,

355
00:24:45,240 --> 00:24:48,980
I would have to travel to that location and pull these cards out

356
00:24:48,981 --> 00:24:50,580
and try to search that information.

357
00:24:50,840 --> 00:24:53,900
One of the great challenges of police work has always been

358
00:24:53,901 --> 00:24:57,220
that we accumulated far more information

359
00:24:57,221 --> 00:25:00,040
than we were ever able to successfully mine ourselves,

360
00:25:00,700 --> 00:25:04,760
that trapped in the file drawers in the cases of police record rooms

361
00:25:04,761 --> 00:25:07,040
were volumes of information that no one could access

362
00:25:07,041 --> 00:25:10,100
without manually just, you know, digging through them.

363
00:25:11,100 --> 00:25:14,920
By the late 1980s, computers were finally being harnessed

364
00:25:14,921 --> 00:25:17,520
to provide rapid access to this wealth of data.

365
00:25:17,840 --> 00:25:21,280
We were able to start using databases and linking those databases

366
00:25:21,281 --> 00:25:22,920
and start doing some analysis.

367
00:25:23,840 --> 00:25:26,220
Computers could not only keep track of individuals,

368
00:25:26,221 --> 00:25:30,700
evidence, and other criminal records, they could also map trends and patterns.

369
00:25:31,500 --> 00:25:34,720
For a long time, the police departments, in order to analyze their data,

370
00:25:34,780 --> 00:25:36,580
they've been sticking pins in a map on a wall.

371
00:25:36,940 --> 00:25:40,540
And now to be able to take that same data, put it on a computer,

372
00:25:41,120 --> 00:25:43,080
graphically present it and analyze it,

373
00:25:43,300 --> 00:25:47,740
that brings a whole new level of sophistication to crime mapping and crime analysis.

374
00:25:48,320 --> 00:25:51,320
We can look for patterns of activity based on MOs,

375
00:25:51,321 --> 00:25:56,100
and we can do that with data as opposed to with the memory of a particular criminal

376
00:25:56,101 --> 00:25:57,065
investigator.

377
00:25:57,066 --> 00:26:02,780
The largest attempt to integrate information on a national level is the NCIC,

378
00:26:03,340 --> 00:26:09,060
the National Crime Information Center. In the 70s, you still had police departments

379
00:26:09,061 --> 00:26:14,720
isolated from each other, and they each began to develop their own methods for tracking police

380
00:26:14,721 --> 00:26:15,421
records.

381
00:26:15,422 --> 00:26:20,260
We have approximately 17,000-plus police forces in the continental United States alone.

382
00:26:20,680 --> 00:26:25,840
It wasn't until the 80s that they really began to look at ways to combine those together, and it

383
00:26:25,841 --> 00:26:27,280
It was driven by the feds.

384
00:26:27,281 --> 00:26:29,240
Federal agencies realized that there was a need

385
00:26:29,241 --> 00:26:30,800
to try to bind those together.

386
00:26:30,801 --> 00:26:32,500
NCIC was the first attempt to do that,

387
00:26:32,501 --> 00:26:34,100
was to combine records.

388
00:26:34,101 --> 00:26:37,420
It's essentially a central repository of information

389
00:26:37,421 --> 00:26:39,900
about criminals throughout the U.S.

390
00:26:39,901 --> 00:26:41,100
They have done their best to integrate

391
00:26:41,101 --> 00:26:43,900
as many state-level databases, criminal records,

392
00:26:43,901 --> 00:26:46,680
into a national computer infrastructure.

393
00:26:46,681 --> 00:26:48,380
It contains fingerprints.

394
00:26:49,280 --> 00:26:52,200
It contains information on missing persons.

395
00:26:52,201 --> 00:26:54,460
It contains information on bodies

396
00:26:54,461 --> 00:26:55,960
that have not been identified.

397
00:26:55,961 --> 00:26:58,420
It contains information on types of crimes

398
00:26:58,421 --> 00:27:00,260
and the MO that has occurred.

399
00:27:01,300 --> 00:27:03,300
For the first time, police departments

400
00:27:03,301 --> 00:27:05,200
throughout the country will have the ability

401
00:27:05,201 --> 00:27:08,620
to share and access the same data.

402
00:27:08,621 --> 00:27:11,260
But remarkable as they are, the one thing computers

403
00:27:11,261 --> 00:27:14,360
have not been able to do is mend a growing rift

404
00:27:14,361 --> 00:27:16,460
between the police and the public.

405
00:27:16,461 --> 00:27:19,860
Coming up, police weapons meet public perception.

406
00:27:19,861 --> 00:27:26,860
Today, the FBI lab investigates over 20,000 cases annually.

407
00:27:26,861 --> 00:27:32,860
One technology that has had an immense impact on how police do their job is television.

408
00:27:32,861 --> 00:27:41,860
In the 1960s, public outcry over images of police using what seemed to be excessive force led to demands for alternatives.

409
00:27:41,861 --> 00:27:43,767
These are citizens. They have rights.

410
00:27:43,768 --> 00:27:46,860
How do we avoid hurting them and what can we use to do that?

411
00:27:46,861 --> 00:27:52,940
And so this has really kind of pushed us into trying to explore alternative ways to subdue people

412
00:27:52,941 --> 00:27:57,440
that will minimize the risk to the officer and minimize the damage to the suspect.

413
00:27:57,441 --> 00:28:00,980
Public perception is a driving force in law enforcement.

414
00:28:01,700 --> 00:28:06,680
We are servants of the community, and as soon as we lose perspective of that, we've already failed.

415
00:28:07,320 --> 00:28:14,440
This problem has led to a long and often frustrating search for what have come to be called less than lethal weapons.

416
00:28:14,441 --> 00:28:20,040
By the mid-1970s, law enforcement was largely limited to very few technologies,

417
00:28:20,041 --> 00:28:24,080
and that was basically the baton, the slapper, and tergass.

418
00:28:24,081 --> 00:28:30,020
New, less-than-lethal weapons, even effective ones, had to stand the critical test of public opinion.

419
00:28:30,021 --> 00:28:32,200
I'll just give you one example.

420
00:28:32,201 --> 00:28:37,300
Back in the 1970s, there was a device marketed called the electric baton.

421
00:28:37,301 --> 00:28:41,960
It was probably one of the more effective devices that we'd ever had,

422
00:28:41,961 --> 00:28:43,720
but it got nicknamed the cattle prod.

423
00:28:44,320 --> 00:28:45,720
We never recovered from that.

424
00:28:46,240 --> 00:28:50,060
To this day, 30 years later, there's no place in the United States

425
00:28:50,061 --> 00:28:52,340
that I'm aware of that's even considering using that

426
00:28:52,341 --> 00:28:56,160
because the public perception is so against it.

427
00:28:56,540 --> 00:28:59,500
Beginning about the 1970s, a few other things came out.

428
00:28:59,620 --> 00:29:03,660
There were things like the taser, which is a device that fires to,

429
00:29:04,520 --> 00:29:09,180
you can think of them as tiny fish hooks that embed themselves in your skin.

430
00:29:09,320 --> 00:29:11,640
You then fire an electrical charge. It can take you down.

431
00:29:11,641 --> 00:29:14,640
I've been shot with a taser. I can tell you exactly what it feels like.

432
00:29:15,420 --> 00:29:16,460
It's not pleasant.

433
00:29:18,060 --> 00:29:21,940
You're going to get hit with about 3 watts of electricity, about 50,000 volts.

434
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:26,640
To me, it felt just like somebody taking his hand and...

435
00:29:27,860 --> 00:29:31,340
Just one after the other, between 8 and 15 times a second.

436
00:29:31,660 --> 00:29:33,640
And your body just starts quivering.

437
00:29:34,120 --> 00:29:39,160
The problem with things like the taser are that the range is limited.

438
00:29:39,161 --> 00:29:43,420
The manufacturer may say 15 feet, but it's 10 to 15 feet.

439
00:29:43,880 --> 00:29:48,700
This only works 85% of the time, which means that 15 times out of 100, that's not going to work.

440
00:29:49,540 --> 00:29:56,520
In the 1980s, the quest for the ideal less-than-lethal weapon was taken up by researchers at the National Institute of Justice.

441
00:29:57,600 --> 00:30:01,940
In 1986, the Attorney General then convened a less-than-lethal conference,

442
00:30:02,460 --> 00:30:06,840
and out of it came a proposal that what we really need is an anesthetic agent,

443
00:30:06,841 --> 00:30:09,800
like an animal dart, how you shoot them at animals

444
00:30:09,801 --> 00:30:12,620
and it puts the animal to sleep and you can carry the animal away.

445
00:30:13,060 --> 00:30:16,280
What was omitted in those discussions and is omitted in the movies

446
00:30:16,281 --> 00:30:19,640
when you see the animal euthanized or anesthetized and moved

447
00:30:19,641 --> 00:30:21,520
is that half of them die in the process.

448
00:30:22,680 --> 00:30:26,260
Another idea was a human glue gun that would shoot sticky foam.

449
00:30:26,960 --> 00:30:30,540
We developed a sticky foam device that can launch sticky foam

450
00:30:30,541 --> 00:30:32,520
for a distance of 35 to 60 feet,

451
00:30:32,620 --> 00:30:34,740
and it really does stick the criminal in place.

452
00:30:34,741 --> 00:30:37,460
and the more he struggles with it, the more it sticks him in place,

453
00:30:37,540 --> 00:30:38,540
but it's got some problems.

454
00:30:39,280 --> 00:30:41,280
One of them is that it's very hard to clean up.

455
00:30:42,040 --> 00:30:44,420
It takes about 10 minutes per square inch.

456
00:30:44,840 --> 00:30:47,120
So then the next question is, how long does it stay sticky?

457
00:30:47,240 --> 00:30:49,200
How long do we have to wait before it's unsticky?

458
00:30:49,560 --> 00:30:51,720
And the answer is a very long time.

459
00:30:51,900 --> 00:30:53,060
Scientific term's eternity.

460
00:30:53,980 --> 00:30:57,780
So we've established unequivocally that this thing that came up in every report

461
00:30:57,781 --> 00:31:00,100
works, but it's not practical.

462
00:31:00,720 --> 00:31:04,520
For a long time, law enforcement, beginning as far back in the 60s,

463
00:31:04,740 --> 00:31:07,580
began to introduce blunt trauma weapons.

464
00:31:07,980 --> 00:31:10,700
These are little beanbags or rubber bullets that you'd shoot,

465
00:31:11,260 --> 00:31:13,340
the notion being you strike them with a rubber bullet.

466
00:31:13,620 --> 00:31:15,840
That reduces the likelihood of a fatality.

467
00:31:17,080 --> 00:31:19,260
But because their trajectories quickly decay,

468
00:31:19,660 --> 00:31:22,480
beanbag rounds only work at very limited ranges.

469
00:31:23,840 --> 00:31:28,520
A more sophisticated blunt trauma weapon is the ring airfoil projectile.

470
00:31:28,860 --> 00:31:31,920
It's basically an airplane wing that's curled into a donut shape,

471
00:31:31,921 --> 00:31:38,520
and then he fires it off a launcher that puts a 5,000 RPM spin on it that actually flies to the target.

472
00:31:39,320 --> 00:31:44,760
This means it can be used at much greater distances to safely but effectively knock the wind out of somebody.

473
00:31:46,080 --> 00:31:52,500
In the late 1970s, police began using pepper spray, a substance found naturally in cayenne peppers.

474
00:31:53,580 --> 00:31:58,700
The first federal agency that ever employed it was the U.S. Post Office,

475
00:31:58,701 --> 00:32:02,840
and they provided them to mailmen.

476
00:32:02,940 --> 00:32:05,220
In fact, while I was in college, I was a mailman briefly

477
00:32:05,221 --> 00:32:06,340
and I actually had some of that.

478
00:32:06,800 --> 00:32:09,140
They used it to keep dogs at bay.

479
00:32:09,640 --> 00:32:11,600
It was used initially to control dogs.

480
00:32:12,160 --> 00:32:15,200
It causes temporary discomfort

481
00:32:15,201 --> 00:32:19,580
and loss of the ability of the dog to tell where the postman is.

482
00:32:19,581 --> 00:32:23,460
So someone, and I don't even know who it was that first came out with it,

483
00:32:23,520 --> 00:32:26,300
said, gee, maybe this could be something that a cop could use.

484
00:32:26,301 --> 00:32:32,760
The first time I ever saw it used was in the 1980s, and it was unbelievably effective.

485
00:32:32,880 --> 00:32:34,160
It was virtually instantaneous.

486
00:32:34,840 --> 00:32:40,280
Pepper spray now comes packed in pepper balls that can be fired using a standard paintball gun.

487
00:32:41,000 --> 00:32:45,300
It gives us the ability of striking a target at greater than the 15 feet that the spray does.

488
00:32:45,560 --> 00:32:49,560
It strikes hard enough to cause a little bit of pain, not enough to cause compliance,

489
00:32:49,760 --> 00:32:53,300
but enough to get your attention, and it hurts, and you involuntarily gasp.

490
00:32:53,301 --> 00:32:54,001
gas.

491
00:32:54,002 --> 00:32:59,880
At the same time it bursts, it releases this cloud of just finely ground pepper dust

492
00:32:59,881 --> 00:33:05,220
and one good whiff and that's all that's in your mind from that point on.

493
00:33:05,221 --> 00:33:10,563
Another device shoots a stream of pepper spray mixed with water called the option.

494
00:33:10,564 --> 00:33:11,280
It attaches

495
00:33:11,281 --> 00:33:16,120
to the barrel of a standard service revolver, giving an officer both the security of a lethal

496
00:33:16,121 --> 00:33:22,240
weapon and also the alternative of using less than lethal force.

497
00:33:22,241 --> 00:33:26,280
Put the gas down!

498
00:33:26,281 --> 00:33:30,240
Another less than lethal weapon still in development is a noxious chemical called

499
00:33:30,241 --> 00:33:32,000
dragon's breath.

500
00:33:32,001 --> 00:33:34,000
This stuff will gag a maggot.

501
00:33:34,001 --> 00:33:40,640
I try to think of some visual way or verbal way of describing it, but words fail me because

502
00:33:40,641 --> 00:33:42,660
unless you smell this stuff at least once.

503
00:33:43,560 --> 00:33:47,100
There's no way I can convey to you how bad stink can be.

504
00:33:48,120 --> 00:33:51,680
One potential use is to stop an agitator from inciting a mob.

505
00:33:52,340 --> 00:33:54,000
We don't have to arrest this individual,

506
00:33:54,160 --> 00:33:57,040
because when we make him stink so bad that he can't influence the crowd,

507
00:33:57,400 --> 00:33:59,640
he's a non-entity. He's no longer a factor.

508
00:34:00,040 --> 00:34:01,980
And he doesn't have to go to jail.

509
00:34:02,180 --> 00:34:03,820
He's probably going to go home and wash,

510
00:34:04,280 --> 00:34:06,140
because he can't stand to be around himself.

511
00:34:06,141 --> 00:34:12,360
Another problem that technology is struggling to solve is the high-speed chase.

512
00:34:13,220 --> 00:34:19,180
To avoid civilian casualties, many departments have greatly restricted officers from engaging in these pursuits.

513
00:34:19,620 --> 00:34:22,240
But a better strategy has been hard to come by.

514
00:34:23,740 --> 00:34:30,260
In the early days of automobiles, police tried out a device designed to hook on to a fleeing vehicle and drag it to a halt.

515
00:34:32,860 --> 00:34:34,360
It never caught on.

516
00:34:34,361 --> 00:34:37,360
A more recent invention is the road spike,

517
00:34:37,361 --> 00:34:40,360
a portable strip that can be rolled across a road

518
00:34:40,361 --> 00:34:42,360
and remotely triggered.

519
00:34:42,361 --> 00:34:44,360
Then you wait for the subject to get there,

520
00:34:44,361 --> 00:34:46,360
then pop it up, take out the subject's tires,

521
00:34:46,361 --> 00:34:49,360
and then put it down before you take out police officer tires.

522
00:34:49,361 --> 00:34:51,360
It would deflate their tires,

523
00:34:51,361 --> 00:34:53,360
hopefully without causing a blowout.

524
00:34:53,361 --> 00:34:55,360
The car would then be forced to go a bit slower,

525
00:34:55,361 --> 00:34:57,360
and so then you could stop the car.

526
00:34:57,361 --> 00:35:01,360
That improves somewhat over years by putting in hollow spikes,

527
00:35:01,361 --> 00:35:03,360
which allow for controlled deflation of the tires

528
00:35:03,361 --> 00:35:07,360
of the tires and dramatically reduce the likelihood of a blowout or loss of control.

529
00:35:07,361 --> 00:35:09,360
The biggest problem is you have to be there first.

530
00:35:09,361 --> 00:35:12,360
And if we knew where the guy was going, why would we chase him to begin with?

531
00:35:12,361 --> 00:35:18,360
And we're going to test every technology that people think can stop a car

532
00:35:18,361 --> 00:35:21,360
so we can determine which really work and which are really safe,

533
00:35:21,361 --> 00:35:24,360
and then we'll let police make those determinations.

534
00:35:24,361 --> 00:35:29,360
In the meantime, we're still looking to try to develop things that would allow you to point something at the car

535
00:35:29,361 --> 00:35:31,360
and zap it and have it stop.

536
00:35:31,361 --> 00:35:34,760
We're nowhere near having that yet, but that's where we'd like to be.

537
00:35:35,480 --> 00:35:38,780
Another area of research involves harnessing the power of light.

538
00:35:39,640 --> 00:35:44,480
The laser dazzler uses a green flashing beam to temporarily blind and disorient.

539
00:35:45,120 --> 00:35:48,700
But the ideal less-than-lethal weapon is still a distant dream.

540
00:35:49,320 --> 00:35:53,400
I think in the future we're going to see some sort of short-range device

541
00:35:53,401 --> 00:35:57,660
that will do the same basic things that a taser will do or that a pepper spray will do

542
00:35:57,661 --> 00:35:59,620
that they can't do right now.

543
00:35:59,621 --> 00:36:06,540
The standard for the perfect non-lethal device is probably the phaser.

544
00:36:07,180 --> 00:36:14,860
The phaser from the Star Trek TV series used a beam of energy to totally disable its victim without causing any bodily harm.

545
00:36:15,620 --> 00:36:20,000
That's the holy grail or the unholy grail, depending on your point of view.

546
00:36:20,460 --> 00:36:25,960
And if you lined up 100 cops, probably 99 of them would say, that's what I really want.

547
00:36:25,961 --> 00:36:27,980
And that is a very, very, very hard thing.

548
00:36:27,981 --> 00:36:33,200
But at the same time, I'm a scientist and I have faith in the future.

549
00:36:33,560 --> 00:36:37,840
And if you look at some of the things that we take for common and granted for today,

550
00:36:38,380 --> 00:36:42,260
you know, five years ago, ten years ago, you say, nah, you never do that.

551
00:36:43,160 --> 00:36:46,140
Coming up, a look at the police car of the future.

552
00:36:47,500 --> 00:36:50,060
TASER stands for Thomas A. Swift's electric rifle

553
00:36:50,061 --> 00:36:53,120
and was named after its inventor's fictional boyhood hero.

554
00:36:53,121 --> 00:37:00,120
In the last few decades, the police car has become a launching pad for new technologies.

555
00:37:00,121 --> 00:37:09,120
It has evolved from being a means of transportation to being a means of rapid response to now

556
00:37:09,121 --> 00:37:12,037
being an information center.

557
00:37:12,038 --> 00:37:19,120
In the 1960s and 70s, police cars went from being off-the-lot models

558
00:37:19,121 --> 00:37:23,320
to customize police packages with improved engine power and durability.

559
00:37:24,040 --> 00:37:27,000
They featured sophisticated multiband radios

560
00:37:27,001 --> 00:37:30,100
that allowed two-way communication not only with dispatch,

561
00:37:30,400 --> 00:37:32,100
but also with other units in the field.

562
00:37:33,560 --> 00:37:37,500
The early 90s saw the addition of a controversial new device,

563
00:37:38,060 --> 00:37:39,400
in-car video cameras.

564
00:37:39,920 --> 00:37:41,240
Police officers don't like the idea

565
00:37:41,241 --> 00:37:44,340
that someone is going to constantly monitor their every activity.

566
00:37:44,341 --> 00:37:45,900
Five years ago, six years ago,

567
00:37:46,180 --> 00:37:47,580
the thought that when I get out of a vehicle

568
00:37:47,581 --> 00:37:49,000
and I go to the window and say,

569
00:37:49,120 --> 00:37:50,620
Sir, can you please hand me your ID?

570
00:37:51,120 --> 00:37:52,840
And that camera looking right at me.

571
00:37:53,780 --> 00:37:54,880
Officers weren't very happy with that.

572
00:37:55,000 --> 00:37:56,160
But now they've seen the benefits.

573
00:37:56,240 --> 00:37:57,420
I think most of them are very happy with it.

574
00:37:58,320 --> 00:38:01,440
In several instances, images captured by in-car video

575
00:38:01,441 --> 00:38:03,880
have provided the critical evidence in police shootings.

576
00:38:03,881 --> 00:38:04,880
Ah!

577
00:38:12,880 --> 00:38:14,380
Radio just being shot!

578
00:38:15,880 --> 00:38:18,760
Another innovation was an in-car computer terminal.

579
00:38:19,120 --> 00:38:24,720
that allowed officers in the field to be directly linked to local, state, even national databases.

580
00:38:25,280 --> 00:38:30,060
An officer can run a tag number on a car and get the registration information on that vehicle,

581
00:38:30,280 --> 00:38:32,500
which would include the owner of the vehicle.

582
00:38:32,940 --> 00:38:37,400
You then can search the database of the owner of the vehicle to see their driving record,

583
00:38:37,540 --> 00:38:39,500
whether or not their license is valid at that time.

584
00:38:39,501 --> 00:38:43,740
Has this person been stopped before and released with a warning?

585
00:38:43,741 --> 00:38:48,180
Does this person have any outstanding parking violations on the car?

586
00:38:48,280 --> 00:38:49,480
And that type of information.

587
00:38:50,380 --> 00:38:54,660
This abundance of technology, valuable as it was, created a new problem.

588
00:38:55,160 --> 00:38:58,200
The cockpit of the car began to get more and more crowded.

589
00:38:59,120 --> 00:39:00,660
And you had this array of switches.

590
00:39:01,180 --> 00:39:03,240
You had switches for the siren, switches for the light,

591
00:39:03,360 --> 00:39:05,000
and you had the radios and radio controls.

592
00:39:05,340 --> 00:39:08,360
Each one was a separate box, and it was incredibly confusing.

593
00:39:09,260 --> 00:39:11,900
There are some people who believe some officers have been killed in traffic

594
00:39:11,901 --> 00:39:14,500
trying to control all of this stuff because it was so hard to do.

595
00:39:14,780 --> 00:39:17,840
To address this problem, the Texas Transportation Institute

596
00:39:17,841 --> 00:39:23,160
began a project for developing a simpler and safer police car called Alert.

597
00:39:24,360 --> 00:39:29,300
We were looking at ways to bring additional technologies into the police vehicle.

598
00:39:29,800 --> 00:39:33,200
The problem we ran into was that there wasn't enough room inside the car

599
00:39:33,201 --> 00:39:34,640
for the equipment that was already there,

600
00:39:34,740 --> 00:39:36,800
and we were talking about putting additional equipment in.

601
00:39:37,140 --> 00:39:40,900
So we felt the first thing we had to do was address the space problem inside the vehicle.

602
00:39:40,901 --> 00:39:47,480
We've moved out, if you will, all the traditional equipment, the overhead light control, the radio, the siren control, the radar.

603
00:39:47,860 --> 00:39:49,720
All of that equipment was moved to the trunk.

604
00:39:50,220 --> 00:39:52,988
The interface for the officer is actually a computer screen.

605
00:39:52,989 --> 00:39:54,280
It's a touchscreen computer.

606
00:39:54,620 --> 00:40:02,720
Now I have one screen, and the screen controls the lights, it controls the siren, it controls my radio frequencies, it controls my video camera.

607
00:40:03,100 --> 00:40:06,840
He sees a violator, somebody exceeding the speed limit.

608
00:40:06,841 --> 00:40:14,140
Instead of using the radar controls and displays, he's using the alert system to control the radar functions and also the display.

609
00:40:14,480 --> 00:40:16,260
Everything would be controlled off that computer.

610
00:40:16,920 --> 00:40:20,100
He now goes into a high-speed pursuit. He tells him I'm going into a pursuit.

611
00:40:20,260 --> 00:40:24,040
The computer decides what the right setting is for the lights and sets them up.

612
00:40:25,900 --> 00:40:33,540
Once he gets the violator stopped, the system can then transmit via GPS the information on his location back to the dispatch area

613
00:40:33,541 --> 00:40:36,440
and have a visual display of the location of the officer.

614
00:40:36,840 --> 00:40:39,080
and all of that done without any input from the officer.

615
00:40:39,720 --> 00:40:42,400
When you exit the car, the cameras will come on for you.

616
00:40:42,500 --> 00:40:43,860
It goes in auto-record mode.

617
00:40:45,020 --> 00:40:49,460
Alert also allows an officer to access this technology when he is away from the car.

618
00:40:49,860 --> 00:40:54,060
What we've done is we've taken the capabilities of the officer inside the car

619
00:40:54,061 --> 00:40:56,100
and moved it outside of the car with handheld computer.

620
00:40:56,101 --> 00:41:00,880
You can then enter information directly from a driver's license into the system.

621
00:41:01,000 --> 00:41:03,300
There's no manual entry necessary by the officer.

622
00:41:03,460 --> 00:41:06,300
This cuts down on the time the officer is on the stop,

623
00:41:06,301 --> 00:41:09,520
and it also increases the accuracy of the information being captured.

624
00:41:09,880 --> 00:41:13,280
There are some areas that are getting into automatic pay.

625
00:41:13,700 --> 00:41:18,900
When an officer makes a traffic stop, he can get a credit card from the individual,

626
00:41:19,320 --> 00:41:25,000
and it will actually deduct the amount of the fine automatically from the person's account.

627
00:41:25,600 --> 00:41:29,700
The alert system is also able to capture and transmit digital imagery.

628
00:41:29,701 --> 00:41:36,520
Currently, if I'm dispatched to a missing child, I go to, say, to the residence of the

629
00:41:36,521 --> 00:41:37,520
parents.

630
00:41:37,521 --> 00:41:38,880
The mother tells me your child's missing.

631
00:41:38,881 --> 00:41:42,500
The first thing I'm going to ask for is a photograph of the child.

632
00:41:42,501 --> 00:41:46,300
At this time, I don't have the capability of distributing that photograph other than

633
00:41:46,301 --> 00:41:47,300
manually.

634
00:41:47,301 --> 00:41:51,140
I have to go get copies made and distribute those copies to officers in the field, and

635
00:41:51,141 --> 00:41:52,720
that's very time consuming.

636
00:41:52,721 --> 00:41:57,580
With this type of system, what we could do is go to the car, capture the image digitally

637
00:41:57,581 --> 00:42:01,200
in the system and then transmit that image directly to the officers in the field.

638
00:42:01,201 --> 00:42:05,340
And we can do that in a matter of minutes rather than a matter of hours.

639
00:42:05,341 --> 00:42:09,880
In the future, such technology could lead to new ways of identifying suspects.

640
00:42:09,881 --> 00:42:14,020
Today, an officer, when he or she stops someone,

641
00:42:14,021 --> 00:42:18,300
we base our information on what that person tells us.

642
00:42:18,301 --> 00:42:21,000
Also through any identification, they care.

643
00:42:21,001 --> 00:42:25,140
You know, and you're really relying on your ability to know whether or not that person

644
00:42:25,141 --> 00:42:26,240
is telling you the truth.

645
00:42:26,241 --> 00:42:28,120
and sometimes that's, you know, difficult to do.

646
00:42:28,620 --> 00:42:30,940
The Arlington County, Virginia Sheriff's Department

647
00:42:30,941 --> 00:42:33,380
now has a computerized mugshot system

648
00:42:33,381 --> 00:42:36,580
capable of identifying someone using only their picture.

649
00:42:37,280 --> 00:42:38,840
After the picture is captured,

650
00:42:39,360 --> 00:42:43,400
we'll plot a number of points on the face, the facial features.

651
00:42:43,960 --> 00:42:47,580
It will take those plotted points and search through the database

652
00:42:47,581 --> 00:42:52,020
to see if that person's picture has ever been taken before

653
00:42:52,021 --> 00:42:53,200
and if it's in our database.

654
00:42:53,800 --> 00:42:55,660
You've got a new scar on your face.

655
00:42:55,661 --> 00:42:58,660
You have a mustache. You change your hairstyle.

656
00:42:59,160 --> 00:43:02,380
It doesn't plot that. It plots the facial structure.

657
00:43:03,440 --> 00:43:06,700
Another portable device allows fingerprints to be captured in the field.

658
00:43:07,240 --> 00:43:09,540
If we were able to collect their fingerprint at the scene,

659
00:43:10,040 --> 00:43:11,560
and we run that through the database,

660
00:43:11,780 --> 00:43:15,580
and if that person's fingerprint is in the database, then we know who we have.

661
00:43:16,560 --> 00:43:21,220
Other new technologies are allowing police to see what until now has been hidden.

662
00:43:21,221 --> 00:43:29,220
Forward-looking infrared radar, or FLIR, uses heat energy to make bodies visible, even in the dark.

663
00:43:29,221 --> 00:43:36,220
Their advantage, of course, is that you can see people who might be hidden behind a bush because the heat will show up,

664
00:43:36,221 --> 00:43:38,196
or maybe they hide in the brush somewhere.

665
00:43:38,197 --> 00:43:40,220
They'll show up because they're a hot spot.

666
00:43:40,221 --> 00:43:47,220
On the horizon are both special magnetic and acoustic technologies, along with infrared technologies,

667
00:43:47,221 --> 00:43:50,220
that can look for a weapon on your body.

668
00:43:50,221 --> 00:43:59,220
Another upcoming technology is a sensor mounted inside a van that can smell faint traces of illegal drugs like crack and PCP.

669
00:44:02,220 --> 00:44:09,220
But ironically, despite this abundance of technology, police work in many places is returning to the days of the beat cop.

670
00:44:09,221 --> 00:44:16,220
The last decade has seen a resurgence of community policing, putting police back in touch with the neighborhoods they serve.

671
00:44:16,221 --> 00:44:22,820
The police department can't control crime and lower the rate of crime on its own.

672
00:44:22,821 --> 00:44:23,820
It needs the help of the community.

673
00:44:23,821 --> 00:44:27,020
And that's what we're there to do, to be in partnership with the community.

674
00:44:27,021 --> 00:44:32,520
At the end of the day, there's nothing more important than our officers' ability to relate

675
00:44:32,521 --> 00:44:35,840
with a diverse human constituency.

676
00:44:35,841 --> 00:44:41,820
That their authority is based as much on their ability to be accepted as a credible person

677
00:44:41,821 --> 00:44:45,220
as it is on the fact that they are wearing a badge and carrying a weapon.

678
00:44:45,221 --> 00:44:48,340
I'm here to help you, okay? I'm not here to beat you up or anything, man.

679
00:44:48,900 --> 00:44:52,800
For all its advances, technology at the dawn of the 21st century

680
00:44:52,801 --> 00:44:58,880
has only accentuated the critical importance of the human being who wears the uniform.

